<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Trudeau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pierre Elliott Trudeau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Trudeau was a Liberal prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1968 to 1979 and from 1980 to 1984. He made English and French official languages, invoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-measures-act">War Measures Act</a> in the October Crisis of 1970 and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the 1980 referendum. In 1982 he secured the patriation of the constitution with the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-trudeau.jpg" alt="Pierre Trudeau" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Press International · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prime_Minister_Pierre_Trudeau_1975_(UPI_press_photo)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Philippe Pierre Yves Elliott Trudeau was born on 18 October 1919 and raised in Outremont, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. He was fluently bilingual from his earliest years. He studied law at the Université de Montréal from 1940 until 1943 and in late 1944 began a master's degree in political economy at Harvard.</p><p>On returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in 1949 he became a leading opponent of Premier Maurice Duplessis and supported the workers in the Asbestos strike. He co-founded and edited the dissident journal Cité Libre, which helped to provide the intellectual basis for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quiet-revolution">Quiet Revolution</a>. Duplessis blacklisted him from teaching law, and from 1949 to 1951 he worked in the Privy Council Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>. In 1965 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> with Gérard Pelletier and Jean Marchand, and he won the Montreal seat of Mount Royal, which he held until 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As minister of justice from 1967 to 1968 he introduced the Criminal Law Amendment Act, which decriminalised homosexual acts between consenting adults and legalised contraception, abortion and lotteries. His energetic campaign, called Trudeaumania, helped him win the Liberal leadership in 1968, and he succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-b-pearson">Lester B. Pearson</a> as prime minister. He led the Liberals to victory in 1968, 1972, 1974 and 1980, losing narrowly in 1979, and served as leader of the Official Opposition from 1979 to 1980.</p><p>His government pioneered official bilingualism and multiculturalism. It established Petro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and launched the National Energy Program, which generated significant controversy in oil-rich Western Canada. He invoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-measures-act">War Measures Act</a> in response to the October Crisis of 1970 and led the federalist campaign in the 1980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> referendum. In foreign policy he established diplomatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and oversaw Canada's entry into the G7 forum. In 1982 he enacted the Constitution Act, which patriated the Constitution and established the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Trudeau retired from politics shortly before the 1984 federal election and practised law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> firm of Heenan Blaikie. He spoke against the Meech Lake and Charlottetown accords, arguing that they would strengthen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> nationalism. He died on 28 September 2000, aged 80. Critics have accused him of arrogance, economic mismanagement and unduly centralising decisions to the detriment of Quebec's culture and the economy of the Prairies, while admirers have praised his intellect and political skill, which they say helped maintain national unity during the Quebec sovereignty movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Trudeau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Front de libération du Québec carried out bombings and, in the October Crisis of 1970, kidnappings that led <b>Pierre Trudeau</b> to invoke the War Measures Act, and the Parti Québécois held referendums on sovereignty in 1980, defeated by about 60 to 40 per cent, and in 1995, defeated…</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-73.59&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Montreal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2804 559 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Trudeau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3254 829h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3254 829h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-73.59&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
